chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 18 of the 3rd edition teaches three plain-text formats for serialised data: CSV, read with csv.reader and written with csv.writer, with DictReader and DictWriter for files that carry a header row, and with a delimiter and a line terminator that can be changed to make tab-separated files; JSON, read with json.loads and written with json.dumps; and XML, read with xml.etree.ElementTree through fromstring, parse, iter and the tag and text attributes, and written with Element, SubElement, set and tostring. It closes with Project 13, which removes the header line from many CSV files, 11 practice questions and the practice program Excel-to-CSV Converter." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's printed behaviours reproduce under Python 3.14.4 when the book's own files are rebuilt: csv.reader gives a list of seven rows of strings for example3.csv and line_num runs from 1 to 7, a second pass over the same reader gives nothing, writerow returns 21, 32 and 16 for the three rows of the first writer example, 21, 30 and 16 for the tab-separated one and 16, 20, 15 and 20 for the DictWriter, json.dumps of the dictionary gives back the JSON text of the chapter character for character, and the XML iteration, filtering and tostring outputs match apart from the differences listed in the next finding." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Four printed outputs of the chapter do not reproduce as printed: the list shown for DictReader on exampleWithHeader3.csv has six dictionaries, but the file holds seven data rows and the first, for 4/5/2035 13:34 Apples 73, is missing from the printed list; the XML text in the chapter's interactive shell contains a typographic closing quotation mark after the namespace address, which the parser rejects as not well-formed at line 3, column 45, and the line breaks of the printed string put a newline into the street and the phone number; the printed tostring result writes the empty car element as /> while Python 3.14.4 writes ' />' with a space before the slash and moves the xmlns:xsi declaration to the root element when the text came from a parse; and the xmltodict output could not be run because the package is not installed in the interpreter used. The chapter also sends the reader to Chapter 18 for HTML although this is Chapter 18, while its other references name Chapter 13 for Beautiful Soup and CSS selectors." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P04 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says the line terminator of a CSV file is a newline by default, but the default of the excel dialect and of csv.writer is the two characters carriage return and line feed, which agrees with RFC 4180; this is why the files written by the chapter's examples hold '\\r\\n' at the end of every row, why writerow counts 21 characters for a row of 19, and why the documentation says a file must be opened with newline='': on a system whose text layer turns '\\n' into '\\r\\n', a file opened without it gets '\\r\\r\\n', which is the double spacing of the chapter's Figure 18-1. On this Linux system the file opened without newline='' came out right; the Windows behaviour was emulated with a text layer that translates the line feed and was not run on Windows." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P08, chx:P14 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Project 13 works as printed when run on three files in a temporary folder: it skips the file that does not end in .csv, writes the two others into headerRemoved without their first line and leaves the originals unchanged, although the introduction of the project says the old contents are replaced while the code writes to a new folder. Its test reader_obj.line_num == 1 finds the header only when the header is one line: the documentation says line_num counts lines read and not records, and a header with a newline inside a quoted name produced a first row whose line_num was 2, so the header stayed in the output; taking the first row with next() does not depend on the line count." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter lists the Python values JSON can hold, and the documentation adds what happens at the edges: a tuple comes back as a list, the keys of a dictionary become strings so that loads(dumps(x)) == x fails for x = {1: 'a'}, a set or a date raises TypeError unless a default function is given, NaN and Infinity are written and read although the specification has no such numbers, a repeated name in an object keeps the last value, non-ASCII characters are escaped unless ensure_ascii is false, a byte order mark at the start raises ValueError, and a file should be opened with encoding='utf-8'." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P07 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Python 3.14 makes python -m json the preferred way to validate and pretty-print JSON, with python -m json.tool still working, and adds exception notes to JSON serialisation errors so that the source of the error can be identified, and the notes of an error for a set nested in a list in a dictionary name the dictionary keys and list items leading to it. Python 3.12 added QUOTE_STRINGS and QUOTE_NOTNULL to csv, so that None can be written unquoted and read back as None; Python 3.11 added tomllib, which reads TOML but has no writing function; since Python 3.12 testing the truth value of an Element emits a DeprecationWarning; and ET.indent, since 3.9, gives indented XML output." ; sen0414:cites chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P04, chx:P06, chx:P11, chx:P13 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 18 is best taught as a choice of format by the shape and the types of the data: CSV for rows with the same columns and only strings back, JSON for nested data with numbers, booleans and null kept, XML for documents with attributes, mixed text and namespaces, with every value text and null only by convention. The printed outputs hold under 3.14.4 except the four listed, and the course should add newline handling and the RFC 4180 terminator, the line_num hazard, the type limits of JSON, exception notes, the command-line tool, quoting modes, tomllib, incremental parsing and the XML security notes. Not executed: the practice program's use of openpyxl and xmltodict, because neither package is installed in the interpreter used, and any Windows behaviour." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P06 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "46 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "14 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-01T12:00:00; 11 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
